From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shreyan Dey <shreyandey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Nov 2026 02:00:00 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 71/71] misc: esp32-regs, a development window onto the S3's
 peripheral registers

Bringing up a peripheral that has no driver yet means writing a handful of
registers and reading back what happened. This kernel has no /dev/mem, and
the reason is written down in the board config: on NOMMU there is no
STRICT_DEVMEM to bound it, so an unrestricted physical window would also
reach core 0's PSRAM. That is the right call for a shipped image and this
patch does not revisit it.

It does leave a gap, and the gap is expensive here. The alternative to a
register window is a kernel patch per guess, and each one costs a full
kernel rebuild plus a hand-entered download mode.

So this is the narrow version of /dev/mem: one device, three windows.
0x60000000-0x6009FFFF is where the S3 puts every peripheral (DR_REG_*_BASE
in the SoC's own reg_base.h), 0x600C0000-0x600C0FFF is the system block
that gates their clocks and holds their resets, and 0x3FC88000-0x3FCFFFFF
is the internal SRAM window, which is handed out as an mmap rather than as
register accesses. None can reach PSRAM. The bounds checks run before
anything is mapped, so a bad address is refused rather than mapped.

The interface is deliberately small:

    read  <addr> [count]   read count 32-bit words (default 1), print hex
    write <addr> <value>   write one 32-bit word

Addresses may be written as 0x6003f000 or as 0x6003f000+0x40c. The offset
form is there because the TRM is written in base+offset and translating by
hand is where mistakes come from. Byte and halfword access are not
supported: every register this is for is 32-bit, and a width parameter
would be one more thing to get wrong.

The mmap window exists for one reason: GDMA requires its descriptor list to
live in internal RAM (TRM 3.4.1), and a descriptor list is memory a program
has to write into, not something it can send through a register. This
kernel is NOMMU, which changes what mmap means -- there is no page table to
install a translation into, so the address a program gets IS the physical
address. mm/nommu.c asks the driver where to site the mapping through
get_unmapped_area(), takes the answer and makes it vma->vm_start
(mm/nommu.c:1148); the driver's mmap hook is then only a check, because on
NOMMU remap_pfn_range() verifies addr == pfn << PAGE_SHIFT and sets
VM_IO|VM_PFNMAP without touching hardware (mm/nommu.c:1594).

All three hooks are required and each fails differently if missing.
mmap_capabilities is how the window is declared writable. Without
get_unmapped_area, mm/nommu.c:755 clears NOMMU_MAP_DIRECT and a MAP_SHARED
request fails with -ENODEV. Without mmap, vfs_mmap() dereferences NULL
(include/linux/fs.h:2061). Userspace must pass MAP_SHARED: MAP_PRIVATE
takes the copy path and would hand back a copy of the window rather than
the window.

This is a development tool. It is off by default, it is not built into the
shipped image, and it hands userspace the ability to hang the board with
one wrong write or one wrong store into the mmap window. That is the trade
it makes, and it is stated in the Kconfig help, in the driver's own header
and in the boot message.

Signed-off-by: Shreyan Dey <shreyandey@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/misc/Kconfig      | 18 ++++++++++++++++++
 drivers/misc/Makefile     |  1 +
 drivers/misc/esp32-regs.c | 471 
 3 files changed, 490 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 drivers/misc/esp32-regs.c

--- a/drivers/misc/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/misc/Kconfig
@@ -634,6 +634,24 @@
 	    - lan966x-miim (MDIO_MSCC_MIIM)
 	    - lan966x-switch (LAN966X_SWITCH)
 
+config ESP32_REGS
+	tristate "ESP32-S3 peripheral register window (development)"
+	depends on OF && HAS_IOMEM
+	help
+	  A character device that lets userspace read and write the ESP32-S3
+	  peripheral register block (0x60000000-0x6009FFFF) and the system
+	  clock/reset block (0x600C0000-0x600C0FFF), and mmap the internal
+	  SRAM window (0x3FC88000-0x3FCFFFFF).
+
+	  This exists because the kernel has no /dev/mem -- on NOMMU there is
+	  no STRICT_DEVMEM to bound it and it would reach core 0's PSRAM -- so
+	  there is otherwise no way to bring up a peripheral that has no
+	  driver yet. It is scoped to those three windows and cannot reach
+	  PSRAM.
+
+	  It is a development tool. A wrong write can hang the board. Say N
+	  unless you are bringing up a peripheral.
+
 config ESP32_IPC
 	tristate "Espressif ESP32 firmware IPC support"
 	depends on OF && HAS_IOMEM

--- a/drivers/misc/Makefile
+++ b/drivers/misc/Makefile
@@ -75,3 +75,4 @@
 obj-y				+= amd-sbi/
 obj-$(CONFIG_MISC_RP1)		+= rp1/
 obj-$(CONFIG_ESP32_IPC)		+= esp32-ipc.o
+obj-$(CONFIG_ESP32_REGS)		+= esp32-regs.o

--- /dev/null
+++ b/drivers/misc/esp32-regs.c
@@ -0,0 +1,486 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
+/*
+ * esp32-regs -- a development window onto the ESP32-S3's peripheral registers.
+ *
+ * Why this exists
+ * ---------------
+ * This kernel has no /dev/mem, and the reason is written down in the board
+ * config: on NOMMU there is no STRICT_DEVMEM to bound it, so an unrestricted
+ * physical window would also reach core 0's PSRAM. That is the right call for
+ * a shipped image and it is not being revisited here.
+ *
+ * It does leave a gap. Bringing up a peripheral that has no driver yet --
+ * GDMA, in this case -- means writing a handful of registers and reading back
+ * what happened, and without a driver there is no way to do that from
+ * userspace. The alternative is a kernel patch per guess, and each one costs a
+ * full rebuild plus a hand-entered download mode.
+ *
+ * So this is the narrow version of /dev/mem: one device, two windows, and
+ * both windows are register space only. 0x60000000-0x6009FFFF is where the
+ * S3 puts every peripheral (DR_REG_*_BASE in the SoC's own reg_base.h) and
+ * 0x600C0000-0x600C0FFF is the system block that gates their clocks and
+ * holds their resets. Neither can reach PSRAM, and neither can reach core
+ * 0's internal SRAM. The bounds check runs before ioremap, so a bad address
+ * is refused rather than mapped.
+ *
+ * This is a development tool. It is off by default (CONFIG_ESP32_REGS), it is
+ * not built into the shipped image, and it hands userspace the ability to hang
+ * the board with one wrong write. That is the trade: it makes the bring-up
+ * work a shell command instead of a rebuild.
+ *
+ * Interface
+ * ---------
+ * A command goes in through write(), and the answer comes back out through
+ * read(). They are separate calls because they are separate operations:
+ *
+ *   echo "read  <addr> [count]" > /dev/esp32-regs
+ *   cat  /dev/esp32-regs
+ *
+ *   echo "write <addr> <value>" > /dev/esp32-regs
+ *
+ * The first version of this returned the answer by copying it back into the
+ * caller's write() buffer. That cannot work: the shell opens the file
+ * write-only, so there is no read() for the answer to arrive on, and the
+ * buffer it wrote into is one the shell has already finished with. The
+ * answer has to come out of read().
+ *
+ * read() returns the result of the last command and then clears it, so a
+ * second cat returns nothing rather than repeating a stale value.
+ *
+ * Addresses are physical and may be given as 0x6003f000 or 0x6003f000+0x40c.
+ * The offset form is there because the datasheet is written in base+offset and
+ * translating by hand is where mistakes come from.
+ *
+ * Deliberately not supported: byte and halfword access. Every register this is
+ * for is 32-bit, and a width parameter would be one more thing to get wrong.
+ *
+ * mmap
+ * ----
+ * The device also hands out a third window, the internal SRAM at
+ * 0x3FC88000-0x3FCFFFFF, and it hands it out as a mapping rather than as
+ * register reads. That window is where the GDMA engine requires its
+ * descriptor list to live (TRM 3.4.1), and a descriptor list is something a
+ * program has to write into memory, not something it can send through a
+ * register. So the device has to be mmap-able.
+ *
+ * This kernel is NOMMU, and that changes what mmap means. There is no page
+ * table to install a translation into: the address a program gets IS the
+ * physical address. mm/nommu.c asks the driver where to site the mapping
+ * through get_unmapped_area(), takes the answer, and makes it vma->vm_start
+ * (mm/nommu.c:1148). The driver's mmap hook is then only a check -- on NOMMU
+ * remap_pfn_range() verifies that addr == pfn << PAGE_SHIFT and sets
+ * VM_IO|VM_PFNMAP (mm/nommu.c:1594), and touches no hardware.
+ *
+ * The three hooks are not optional and each has a distinct failure mode:
+ *
+ *   .mmap_capabilities  without it a chardev gets NOMMU_MAP_DIRECT by default,
+ *                       but naming it is how the window is declared writable.
+ *   .get_unmapped_area  without it mm/nommu.c:755 clears NOMMU_MAP_DIRECT and
+ *                       a MAP_SHARED request fails with -ENODEV.
+ *   .mmap               vfs_mmap() calls file->f_op->mmap with no NULL check
+ *                       (include/linux/fs.h:2061), so a device that reports
+ *                       NOMMU_MAP_DIRECT without one dereferences NULL.
+ *
+ * Userspace must ask for MAP_SHARED. MAP_PRIVATE takes the copy path in
+ * mm/nommu.c and would hand back a copy of the window rather than the window.
+ *
+ * The window is a mapping, so a program can hang the board with it just as
+ * easily as with a register write. That is the same trade as the rest of this
+ * device and it is why the device is off by default.
+ */
+
+#include <linux/io.h>
+#include <linux/kernel.h>
+#include <linux/miscdevice.h>
+#include <linux/module.h>
+#include <linux/mutex.h>
+#include <linux/fs.h>
+#include <linux/mm.h>
+#include <linux/slab.h>
+#include <linux/uaccess.h>
+
+#define DRV_NAME	"esp32-regs"
+
+/*
+ * The whole peripheral register block. DR_REG_GDMA_BASE is 0x6003F000 and
+ * DR_REG_SYSTEM_BASE is 0x600C0000, both inside this span; the S3's highest
+ * peripheral base is below 0x600A0000.
+ */
+#define PERIPH_BASE	0x60000000
+#define PERIPH_END	0x600A0000
+
+/*
+ * The system block: peripheral clock gates and reset lines live here, and so
+ * does the external-memory cache controller at 0x600C4000.
+ *
+ * The cache block is not in the TRM's register map -- Table 4.3-3 lists
+ * 0x600C_4000-0x600C_BFFF as Reserved -- but the silicon has it and ESP-IDF
+ * drives it: components/soc/esp32s3/include/soc/reg_base.h defines
+ * DR_REG_EXTMEM_BASE as 0x600C4000, and components/esp_system/port/cpu_start.c
+ * calls Cache_Enable_DCache() at startup, on core 0, before this kernel runs.
+ * That is why the window reaches it: Linux inherits a live write-back L1
+ * data cache over PSRAM, and the only way to flush it from this side is
+ * EXTMEM_DCACHE_SYNC_CTRL_REG at 0x600C4028.
+ */
+#define SYS_BASE	0x600C0000
+#define SYS_END		0x600C5000
+
+/*
+ * The internal SRAM window, mmap-able. GDMA's descriptor list has to live in
+ * internal RAM (TRM 3.4.1), and this 416 KB span is the part of internal RAM
+ * that is not the ROM, not the RTC block and not the region the ROM loader
+ * uses. Linux's own memory is entirely external PSRAM (0x3d800000 for 8 MB),
+ * so nothing here is memory the kernel is using.
+ */
+#define SRAM_BASE	0x3FC88000
+#define SRAM_END	0x3FD00000
+
+#define MAX_WORDS	64
+#define LINE_MAX	160
+/*
+ * A read of MAX_WORDS words prints one line per word, and each line is
+ * "0x%08lx 0x%08x\n" -- 10 + 1 + 10 + 1 = 22 bytes. The first version of this
+ * sized the buffer at 16 bytes a line and silently dropped the last eight
+ * words of a 64-word read. 24 leaves room for a line that grows.
+ */
+#define LINE_OUT	24
+#define RESULT_MAX	(MAX_WORDS * LINE_OUT + 1)
+
+/*
+ * The answer to the last command, waiting to be read. One buffer, because
+ * one command is outstanding at a time; the mutex keeps a writer and a
+ * reader from interleaving and hands each a consistent view.
+ */
+static char esp32_regs_result[RESULT_MAX];
+static size_t esp32_regs_result_len;
+static DEFINE_MUTEX(esp32_regs_lock);
+
+static bool sram_allowed(unsigned long addr, size_t len);
+
+static bool addr_allowed(unsigned long addr, size_t len)
+{
+	if (addr >= PERIPH_BASE && addr + len <= PERIPH_END)
+		return true;
+	if (addr >= SYS_BASE && addr + len <= SYS_END)
+		return true;
+	return sram_allowed(addr, len);
+}
+
+/*
+ * The internal SRAM window. It is reachable two ways: handed to userspace
+ * whole through mmap, and -- since the GDMA bring-up -- read and written a
+ * word at a time through the register commands, so a descriptor the engine
+ * has just rejected can be read back and compared against what was written.
+ * Without that, a descriptor error is a dead end: the engine says it refused
+ * something and there is no way to look at the something.
+ *
+ * The overflow check is not decoration. addr + len wraps for a large enough
+ * addr, and a wrapped sum passes a naive upper-bound test.
+ */
+static bool sram_allowed(unsigned long addr, size_t len)
+{
+	if (len == 0)
+		return false;
+	if (addr < SRAM_BASE)
+		return false;
+	if (addr > SRAM_END || len > SRAM_END - addr)
+		return false;
+	return true;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Parse "0x6003f000" or "0x6003f000+0x40c". Returns 0 on success and leaves
+ * the resolved physical address in *out.
+ */
+static int parse_addr(const char *s, unsigned long *out)
+{
+	unsigned long base, off = 0;
+	char *end;
+
+	base = simple_strtoul(s, &end, 0);
+	if (end == s)
+		return -EINVAL;
+	if (*end == '+') {
+		const char *p = end + 1;
+
+		off = simple_strtoul(p, &end, 0);
+		if (end == p)
+			return -EINVAL;
+	}
+	if (*end != '\0' && *end != '\n')
+		return -EINVAL;
+	*out = base + off;
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static ssize_t esp32_regs_write(struct file *file, const char __user *ubuf,
+				size_t count, loff_t *ppos)
+{
+	char *buf;
+	char *cmd, *arg1, *arg2;
+	unsigned long addr, value;
+	void __iomem *map;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (count == 0 || count > LINE_MAX)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	buf = memdup_user_nul(ubuf, count);
+	if (IS_ERR(buf))
+		return PTR_ERR(buf);
+
+	/* Strip the trailing newline the shell always sends. */
+	buf[strcspn(buf, "\n")] = '\0';
+
+	cmd = strstrip(buf);
+	arg1 = strsep(&cmd, " \t");
+	arg2 = cmd ? strstrip(cmd) : NULL;
+
+	if (!arg1 || !*arg1) {
+		ret = -EINVAL;
+		goto out;
+	}
+
+	if (!strcmp(arg1, "read")) {
+		unsigned long n = 1;
+		char *p;
+		u32 words[MAX_WORDS];
+		size_t i, pos = 0;
+
+		if (!arg2 || !*arg2) {
+			ret = -EINVAL;
+			goto out;
+		}
+		p = strsep(&arg2, " \t");
+		if (parse_addr(p, &addr)) {
+			ret = -EINVAL;
+			goto out;
+		}
+		if (arg2 && *arg2) {
+			n = simple_strtoul(strstrip(arg2), NULL, 0);
+			if (n == 0 || n > MAX_WORDS) {
+				ret = -EINVAL;
+				goto out;
+			}
+		}
+		if (!addr_allowed(addr, n * sizeof(u32))) {
+			ret = -EINVAL;
+			goto out;
+		}
+
+		map = ioremap(addr, n * sizeof(u32));
+		if (!map) {
+			ret = -ENOMEM;
+			goto out;
+		}
+		for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
+			words[i] = ioread32(map + i * sizeof(u32));
+		iounmap(map);
+
+		/*
+		 * One line per word, address first, so the output can be
+		 * diffed between runs and pasted into a bug report.
+		 */
+		mutex_lock(&esp32_regs_lock);
+		for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
+			int len = scnprintf(esp32_regs_result + pos,
+					    RESULT_MAX - pos,
+					    "0x%08lx 0x%08x\n",
+					    addr + i * sizeof(u32), words[i]);
+
+			if (len <= 0 || pos + len >= RESULT_MAX)
+				break;
+			pos += len;
+		}
+		esp32_regs_result_len = pos;
+		mutex_unlock(&esp32_regs_lock);
+
+		ret = count;
+		goto out;
+	}
+
+	if (!strcmp(arg1, "write")) {
+		char *p;
+
+		if (!arg2 || !*arg2) {
+			ret = -EINVAL;
+			goto out;
+		}
+		p = strsep(&arg2, " \t");
+		if (parse_addr(p, &addr)) {
+			ret = -EINVAL;
+			goto out;
+		}
+		if (!arg2 || !*arg2) {
+			ret = -EINVAL;
+			goto out;
+		}
+		value = simple_strtoul(strstrip(arg2), NULL, 0);
+		if (!addr_allowed(addr, sizeof(u32))) {
+			ret = -EINVAL;
+			goto out;
+		}
+
+		map = ioremap(addr, sizeof(u32));
+		if (!map) {
+			ret = -ENOMEM;
+			goto out;
+		}
+		iowrite32((u32)value, map);
+		iounmap(map);
+
+		/*
+		 * A write has no value to report, but it must clear any
+		 * stale answer, or the next cat would return the previous
+		 * command's result and look like this write's.
+		 */
+		mutex_lock(&esp32_regs_lock);
+		esp32_regs_result_len = 0;
+		mutex_unlock(&esp32_regs_lock);
+
+		ret = count;
+		goto out;
+	}
+
+	/* Anything else is a typo, and saying so is more useful than -EINVAL. */
+	ret = -EINVAL;
+
+out:
+	kfree(buf);
+	return ret;
+}
+
+static ssize_t esp32_regs_read(struct file *file, char __user *ubuf,
+			       size_t count, loff_t *ppos)
+{
+	ssize_t ret;
+
+	if (*ppos != 0)
+		return 0;
+
+	mutex_lock(&esp32_regs_lock);
+	if (esp32_regs_result_len == 0) {
+		mutex_unlock(&esp32_regs_lock);
+		return 0;
+	}
+	if (count > esp32_regs_result_len)
+		count = esp32_regs_result_len;
+	if (copy_to_user(ubuf, esp32_regs_result, count)) {
+		mutex_unlock(&esp32_regs_lock);
+		return -EFAULT;
+	}
+	/* Consumed: a second cat must not repeat it. */
+	esp32_regs_result_len = 0;
+	ret = count;
+	mutex_unlock(&esp32_regs_lock);
+
+	*ppos += ret;
+	return ret;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Where the mapping goes. On NOMMU the answer is the physical address itself:
+ * mm/nommu.c takes whatever this returns and makes it vma->vm_start, so the
+ * program's pointer and the hardware address are the same number. That is the
+ * whole mechanism -- there is no translation to set up.
+ *
+ * pgoff is in pages and is the offset the program passed to mmap(), so a
+ * program can map a sub-range of the window by passing an offset.
+ */
+static unsigned long esp32_regs_get_unmapped_area(struct file *file,
+						  unsigned long addr,
+						  unsigned long len,
+						  unsigned long pgoff,
+						  unsigned long flags)
+{
+	unsigned long phys = SRAM_BASE + (pgoff << PAGE_SHIFT);
+
+	if (!sram_allowed(phys, len))
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	return phys;
+}
+
+/*
+ * NOMMU_MAP_DIRECT is what makes MAP_SHARED possible at all; without it
+ * mm/nommu.c:764 refuses the request with -ENODEV. READ and WRITE are both
+ * named because a descriptor list is written, not just read.
+ */
+static unsigned esp32_regs_mmap_capabilities(struct file *file)
+{
+	return NOMMU_MAP_DIRECT | NOMMU_MAP_READ | NOMMU_MAP_WRITE;
+}
+
+/*
+ * The hook itself. On NOMMU remap_pfn_range() does not install anything -- it
+ * checks that addr == pfn << PAGE_SHIFT and sets VM_IO|VM_PFNMAP, which is
+ * what tells the rest of the kernel this range is device memory and must not
+ * be treated as a normal page. The identity it checks is exactly the one
+ * get_unmapped_area() established, so it holds.
+ *
+ * Wrapping the SRAM window in VM_IO is the point, not a formality: it is what
+ * stops the kernel from faulting these addresses in as ordinary anonymous
+ * pages. The address is the same either way; the flag is what makes the
+ * access go to the SRAM rather than to a page fault.
+ */
+static int esp32_regs_mmap(struct file *file, struct vm_area_struct *vma)
+{
+	unsigned long len = vma->vm_end - vma->vm_start;
+	unsigned long phys = SRAM_BASE + (vma->vm_pgoff << PAGE_SHIFT);
+
+	if (!sram_allowed(phys, len))
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	/*
+	 * The window is internal SRAM, not a peripheral FIFO, so the normal
+	 * cacheable attribute is correct. pgprot_noncached() is what the
+	 * register windows would want if they were mmap-able; they are not.
+	 */
+	return remap_pfn_range(vma, vma->vm_start, phys >> PAGE_SHIFT,
+			       len, vma->vm_page_prot);
+}
+
+static const struct file_operations esp32_regs_fops = {
+	.owner		= THIS_MODULE,
+	.read		= esp32_regs_read,
+	.write		= esp32_regs_write,
+	.llseek		= noop_llseek,
+	.mmap		= esp32_regs_mmap,
+	.get_unmapped_area = esp32_regs_get_unmapped_area,
+	.mmap_capabilities = esp32_regs_mmap_capabilities,
+};
+
+static struct miscdevice esp32_regs_misc = {
+	.minor	= MISC_DYNAMIC_MINOR,
+	.name	= DRV_NAME,
+	.fops	= &esp32_regs_fops,
+	.mode	= 0600,
+};
+
+static int __init esp32_regs_init(void)
+{
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = misc_register(&esp32_regs_misc);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	pr_info(DRV_NAME ": /dev/%s ready; window 0x%08x-0x%08x and 0x%08x-0x%08x\n",
+		DRV_NAME, PERIPH_BASE, PERIPH_END, SYS_BASE, SYS_END);
+	pr_info(DRV_NAME ": mmap window 0x%08x-0x%08x (internal SRAM)\n",
+		SRAM_BASE, SRAM_END);
+	pr_warn(DRV_NAME ": development tool; a wrong write can hang the board\n");
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static void __exit esp32_regs_exit(void)
+{
+	misc_deregister(&esp32_regs_misc);
+}
+
+module_init(esp32_regs_init);
+module_exit(esp32_regs_exit);
+
+MODULE_AUTHOR("Shreyan Dey");
+MODULE_DESCRIPTION("Development register window for the ESP32-S3 peripherals");
+MODULE_LICENSE("GPL");
